\abstract{Given an undirected graph $G=(V,E)$, a vertex $v\in V$ is edge-vertex (ev) dominated by an edge $e\in E$ if $v$ is either incident to $e$ or incident to an adjacent edge of $e$. A set $S^{ev}\subseteq E$ is an edge-vertex dominating set (referred to as \textit{ev}-dominating set and in short as \textit{EVDS}) of $G$ if every vertex of $G$ is \textit{ev}-dominated by at least one edge of $S^{ev}$. The minimum cardinality of an \textit{ev}-dominating set is the \textit{ev}-domination number. The edge-vertex dominating set problem is to find a minimum \textit{ev}-domination number. 
In this paper we prove that the \textit{ev}-dominating set problem is {\tt NP-hard} on unit disk graphs. We also prove that this problem admits a polynomial-time approximation scheme on unit disk graphs. Finally, we give a simple 5-factor linear-time approximation algorithm. 
% \textcolor{red}{REMOVE? We also present a case study demonstrating the relevance of the \textit{EVDS} model in mining engineering. In particular, we discuss an application of the model for optimal distribution of multiple facility centers for maintenance and disaster management in the mining area. Here, the \textit{EVDS} places a minimum number of centers close to mining sites but in safe places.}
}

