%In the literature, analogous to the edge-vertex dominating set there is a variant of the dominating set called vertex-edge dominating set, which too is studied on unit disk graphs \cite{jena2020}. Given an undirected graph $G=(V,E)$, an edge $e\in E$ is \textit{ve (vertex-edge)-dominated} by a vertex $v\in V$ if $e$ is incident on $v$ (i.e., an end point of $e$) or if $e$ is incident on some adjacent vertex of $v$. A set $S^{ve}\subseteq V$ is an \textit{vertex-edge dominating (VED)} set (referred to as \textit{ve-dominating set}) of $G$, if at least one vertex of $S^{ve}$ (at least two vertices for \textit{double vertex-edge dominating set}), \textit{ve-dominates} every edge of $G$. The minimum cardinality of an \textit{ve-dominating set} is the \textit{ve-domination number}, denoted by $\gamma_{ve}(G)$.
