113:\abstract{Given an undirected graph $G=(V,E)$, a vertex $v\in V$ is edge-vertex (ev) dominated by an edge $e\in E$ if $v$ is either incident to $e$ or incident to an adjacent edge of $e$. A set $S^{ev}\subseteq E$ is an edge-vertex dominating set (referred to as \textit{ev}-dominating set and in short as \textit{EVDS}) of $G$ if every vertex of $G$ is \textit{ev}-dominated by at least one edge of $S^{ev}$. The minimum cardinality of an \textit{ev}-dominating set is the \textit{ev}-domination number. The edge-vertex dominating set problem is to find a minimum \textit{ev}-domination number. 
114:In this paper we prove that the \textit{ev}-dominating set problem is {\tt NP-hard} on unit disk graphs. We also prove that this problem admits a polynomial-time approximation scheme on unit disk graphs. Finally, we give a simple 5-factor linear-time approximation algorithm. 
130:\keywords{Unit disk graph, Edge-vertex dominating set, Approximation algorithm, Edge-facility center location}%, Disaster management}
138:\section{Introduction}\label{intro}
141:Given an undirected graph $G=(V,E)$, a vertex $v\in V$ is \textit{ev (edge-vertex)-dominated} by an edge $e\in E$ if $v$ is incident to $e$ (i.e., an endpoint of $e$) or if $v$ is incident to an adjacent edge of $e$. A set $S^{ev}\subseteq E$ is an \textit{edge-vertex dominating set (EVDS)} (referred to as \textit{ev-dominating set}) of $G$ if every vertex of $G$ is $ev$-dominated by at least one edge of $S^{ev}$ (at least two edges for \textit{double edge-vertex dominating set}). The minimum cardinality of an \textit{ev-dominating set} is the \textit{ev-domination number}, denoted by $\gamma_{ev}(G)$. 
142:A \textit{paired-dominating set (PDS)} of a graph $G(V,E)$ with no isolated vertices is a dominating set $S^{pr}\subseteq V$ and a sub-graph induced by $S^{pr}$ in $G$ have a perfect matching. The minimum cardinality of a \textit{PDS} of $G$ is symbolized as $\gamma_{pr}(G)$. Note that \textit{EVDS} and \textit{PDS} may be completely different subsets of edges in the same graph, and their cardinalities may always be equal (see Fig. \ref{pds}). In Figure \ref{pds}, the blue colored edges represent the {\it EVDS}. However, it should be noted that this set does not fulfill the criteria to be classified as a {\it PDS}; instead, it could correspond to the set of green edges.
143:% \textcolor{red}{ If $S^{ev}$ is an edge-vertex dominating set and every edge in $S^{ev}$ has an endpoint with another edge in $S^{ev}$, then $S^{ev}$ is called the \textit{total edge-vertex dominating} set, and the minimum cardinality of this set is called the \textit{total edge-vertex domination number}, and it is denoted by $\gamma^t_{ev}$}. 
144:Another similar model, called {\it total domination} in a graph, is defined in terms of only vertices instead of \textit{edge-vertex}. A {\it total dominating set} ({\it TDS}) of a graph $G=(V,E)$ is a {\it dominating set} $S^d\subseteq V$ such that every vertex $v\in S^d$ is adjacent to a vertex. The {\it TDS} problem is to find such a set $S^d$ of minimum cardinality. The cardinality of minimum {\it TDS} is denoted by $\gamma_t$. We can also view a {\it TDS} in a graph as a minimum cardinality set of pairs of adjacent vertices (hence, as a set of edges induced on these vertices), where these pairs may share a common vertex. Therefore, a {\it TDS} is also an {\it EVDS} and vice versa. However, a minimum cardinality {\it EVDS} may not be a minimum cardinality {\it TDS} i.e., the set of all vertices incident to edges of a minimum cardinality {\it EVDS} may not necessarily form a minimum cardinality {\it TDS}, and vice versa (see Fig. \ref{tv}). In Figure \ref{tv}, the blue edges represent the minimum cardinality {\it EVDS}, while the red vertices represent the minimum cardinality {\it TDS}. Here we can observe that the cardinality of the vertices incident to blue edges exceeds the minimum cardinality {\it TDS} and the cardinality of the edges incident to the red vertices also exceeds the minimum cardinality {\it EVDS}. \\
146:%In the literature, analogous to the edge-vertex dominating set, there is a variant of the dominating set called \textit{vertex-edge (ve) dominating set (VEDS)}. Another similar domination model is the \textit{connected domination} model. However, these models are different from \textit{ev-domination}. A \textit{VEDS} is a set of vertices \textit{ve}-dominating all the edges, whereas \textit{EVDS} is a set of edges \textit{ev}-dominating all the vertices. We distinguish between \textit{connected domination} and \textit{edge-vertex domination} models with counter-examples below. A \textit{connected dominating set ({\it CDS})} of a graph $G=(V, E)$ is defined as the set of vertices such that every vertex $v\in V$ is either in {\it CDS} or adjacent to vertices of {\it CDS}, and all the vertices in {\it CDS} are connected. The minimum {\it CDS} problem is to find the minimum cardinality of such a set (see Fig. \ref{ex1}(c)). As defined above, an edge-vertex dominating set ({\it EVDS}) is a set of edges such that every $v\in V$ is incident to an edge $e$ in {\it EVDS} or adjacent to a vertex that is incident to an edge $e$ in {\it EVDS}. The {\it EVDS} problem is to find the minimum cardinality of such a set (see Fig. \ref{ex1}(b)).
148:% The minimum cardinality {\it EVDS} need not be part of {\it CDS} (see Fig. \ref{ex1}). A connected dominating set  (taken as a collection of induced edges) is an edge vertex dominating set (though not necessarily minimum {\it EVDS}), but vice versa need not be true.
155:% \caption{(a) Graph $G(V,E)$ (b) The subset $\{\overline{cd},\overline{gi}\}\in E$ is the minimum edge-vertex dominating set of $G$  (c) The subset $\{a,d,e,f,j\}\in V$ is the minimum connected dominating set of $G$} \label{ex1}
163:% \caption{(a) Graph $G(V,E)$, (b) The subset $\{(c, d),(g,i)\}\subseteq E$ is the minimum edge-vertex dominating set of $G$,  (c) The subset $\{a,d,e,f,j\}\subseteq V$ is the minimum connected dominating set of $G$.} \label{ex1}
167:%In the literature, analogous to the edge-vertex dominating set there is a variant of the dominating set called vertex-edge dominating set, which too is studied on unit disk graphs \cite{jena2020}. Given an undirected graph $G=(V,E)$, an edge $e\in E$ is \textit{ve (vertex-edge)-dominated} by a vertex $v\in V$ if $e$ is incident on $v$ (i.e., an end point of $e$) or if $e$ is incident on some adjacent vertex of $v$. A set $S^{ve}\subseteq V$ is an \textit{vertex-edge dominating (VED)} set (referred to as \textit{ve-dominating set}) of $G$, if at least one vertex of $S^{ve}$ (at least two vertices for \textit{double vertex-edge dominating set}), \textit{ve-dominates} every edge of $G$. The minimum cardinality of an \textit{ve-dominating set} is the \textit{ve-domination number}, denoted by $\gamma_{ve}(G)$.
185:The edge-vertex dominating set problem may have the following potential applications:
193:% The applications of the edge-vertex dominating set problem arise in locating the monitoring devices such as cameras to safeguard facilities in a given region. We can place the monitoring devices at the endpoints of an edge that is in the edge-vertex dominating set. Hence, these monitoring devices can monitor all the adjacent facilities, including the monitoring devices, which are at another endpoint of the edge that is in the edge-vertex dominating set. If one of the devices fails, then the immediate adjacent device can service those facilities that are monitored by the failed device. In the following we discuss one specific application of the \textit{EVDS} problem.
206:% We can model a solution to this as follows: consider each mining machine as a graph node, there is an edge between two machines if they are at most some distance $d$ apart. We want the distance between every mining machine to the nearest base station to be at most $1.5d$ (i.e., the maximum distance between a base station and a mining machine). Here, a pair of mining spots separated by a distance of at most $\mu d$ needs to be taken as a single spot if having a center between them happens to be so close to both of them that a small mishap at one of them will damage both the center and pieces of machinery at the spots, where $\mu$, a real number close to $0$, is chosen by practitioners such as geoengineers/geoscientists. We can consider this graph as a unit disk graph by setting $d=1$ after appropriate scaling. Find this graph's minimum cardinality edge-vertex dominating set ($S^{ev}$). The base stations need to be established such that they are away from the mining machines to avoid the nuisance generated by these machines. That is, every facility center is away from a mining spot by at least $0.5\mu d$ distance (see Fig. \ref{min} where a facility located at $b_k$ is at least $0.5\mu d$ distance from the mining spots $m_i$, $m_j$ and a possible spot $m_h$) and every mining spot receives service from a facility center lying within $1.5d$ distance from it. Hence, it is feasible to establish these base stations at the midpoint of each edge $e\in S^{ev}$. The parameters $\mu$ and $d$ are to be appropriately tuned by practitioners based on their expertise, proposed mining process, type of air-drop facility, and local conditions. Apart from the above application, other disaster management such as army resource supply management, setting up of emergency medical centers in rural areas during calamities, etc., may use \textit{EVDS} model and algorithmic solutions developed in this paper (e.g., see the case study in \cite{sh2019}).}
216:%  Suppose that we have a unit disk graph (as given in Fig. \ref{figtev}) and that we select the green-colored edges forming an {\it EVDS}  to place the facility centers. However, the endpoints of the green edges also create another {\it TDS} but may be of a larger cardinality. Let the vertices of a minimum cardinality {\it TDS} be blue-colored vertices. Due to the nature of the hazardous situation, the facility centers need to be as scattered as possible. This dispersion of the centers is desirable as they have to provide services covering a maximum geographical area. At the same time, there should be minimal impact on the nearby centers when any disaster occurs at one of the facility centers. In fact, in our {\it PTAS} (in Section \ref{poly}), the local construction algorithm selects the edges for {\it EVDS} such that they are as much scattered as possible by computing a maximal matching (maximal independent edge set) for each graph in a 4-separated collection of subgraphs (the green-colored edges in Fig. \ref{figtev}).}
231:\section{Related Work}
232:The {\it edge-vertex dominating set} and {\it vertex-edge dominating set} in a graph was introduced by Peters \cite{pete1987}. The edge-vertex dominating set and vertex-edge dominating set problems are {\tt NP-complete}, even when restricted to bipartite graphs \cite{lewi2007}. For every nontrivial tree ${\cal T}$, an upper bound on $\gamma_{ev}({\cal T})$ is $(\gamma_{t}({\cal T})+s-1)/2$ where $s$ is the number of support vertices (the vertex adjacent to a leaf) \cite{venk2018}. The \textit{total domination number} ($\gamma_t$) of a tree is equal to the \textit{ev-domination number} ($\gamma_{ev}$) plus one \cite{kris2016}. The vertex-edge dominating set problem in {\it UDG} is {\tt NP-complete} \cite{jena2020}. Also, in \cite{jena2020}, a polynomial time approximation scheme ({\tt PTAS}) is proposed. Finding $\gamma_{ve}$ even in cubic planar graphs is {\tt NP-hard} \cite{ziem2020}. The vertex-edge domination problem can be solved in linear time on block graphs \cite{paul2019}. In the same paper, it is also shown that finding $\gamma_{ve}$ in undirected path graphs is {\tt NP-complete}. Given a connected graph $G$ with $n$ vertices where $n\ge 6$, then we have $\gamma_{ve}(G)\leq \lfloor \frac{n}{3} \rfloor$\cite{zyli2019}. Boutrig et al. \cite{bout2016} gave an upper bound for the independent ve-domination number in terms of the ve-domination number for connected $K_{1,k}$-free graph with $k\geq 3$ and also gave an upper bound on the ve-domination number for connected $C_5$-free graph.  
234:The double vertex-edge domination was introduced by Krishnakumari et al. \cite{kris2017}. They showed that finding $\gamma_{dve}$ in a bipartite graph is {\tt NP-complete} and also proved that for every non-trivial connected graph $G$, $\gamma_{dve}(G)\geq \gamma_{ve}(G)+1$, and $\gamma_{dve}({\cal T})=\gamma_{ve}({\cal T})+1$ or $\gamma_{dve}({\cal T})=\gamma_{ve}({\cal T})+2$ for any tree ${\cal T}$. Finding $\gamma_{dve}$ in chordal graphs is {\tt NP-complete} \cite{venk2019}. They gave a linear time algorithm to find $\gamma_{dve}$ in proper interval graphs and also showed that finding $\gamma_{dve}$ in general graphs with vertices having a degree at most 5 is {\tt APX-complete}. The double version of edge-vertex domination was studied by Sahin and Sahin \cite{sahi2021}. They also gave the relationship between $\gamma_{dev}$ and $\gamma_{dve}$, $\gamma_{t}$, $\gamma_{ev}$ for trees and graphs, and also gave formulas to determine the \textit{double ev-domination number} of paths and cycles. Sahin and Sahin \cite{SAHI2020} proved that the total ev-dominating set problem is {\tt NP-hard} for bipartite graphs. They also showed that $(n-l+2s-1)/2$ is the upper bound for $\gamma^t_{ev}$ for a tree ${\cal T}$ with order $n$, $l$ leaves and $s$ supporting vertices. To the best of our knowledge, in the literature, the ev-dominating set problem is not yet studied in the context of geometric intersection graphs. 
236:\subsection{Our Contribution}
237:In this article, we study the {\it EVDS} problem on unit disk graphs. We show that the decision version of this problem is {\tt NP-complete} in UDGs. We also prove that this problem on UDG admits a polynomial time approximation scheme (PTAS). We finally present a simple 5-factor linear-time approximation algorithm.
241:\section{Hardness Results}
242:In this section, we show that the decision version of the {\it EVDS} is {\tt NP-complete}, as stated below. We describe a polynomial time reduction from the vertex cover problem, which is known to be {\tt NP-complete} in planar graphs with maximum degree 3 \cite{grey1979}, to {\it EVDS} problem on {\it UDG}. \\
246:\textbf{Question:} Does there exist an edge-vertex dominating set $S^{ev}$ of $G$ such that $\vert S^{ev}\vert \leq k$.\\
248:\begin{lemma}(\cite{vali1981})\label{lem1} 
254:\begin{corollary}(\cite{jena2020})\label{cor1} An embedding of a planar graph $G=(V,E)$ with $\lvert V \rvert \geq3$ and maximum degree 3 in the plane can be constructed in polynomial time, where the embedding is such that the vertices of $G$ are at $(4i,4j)$ and the edges of $G$ are drawn as a sequence of consecutive line segments along the lines $x=4i$ or $y=4j$, for some $i$ and $j$.
264:\begin{lemma}\label{lem2}
265:Let $G=(V,E)$ be an instance of the vertex cover problem with a number of edges at least 2 and a maximum degree 3. An instance $G'=(V',E')$ of {\it EVDS-UDG}  can be constructed from $G$ in polynomial time.
271:Let $A$ be the set of added points and $J$ be the set of joint points. We construct a UDG $G'=(V',E')$  where the vertex set $V'=V\cup A\cup J$, and there is an edge between two vertices of $V'$ if and only if the distance between them is at most 1 unit (see Fig. \ref{fig2}). If $l$ is the total number of line segments in the embedding, then $\lvert A \rvert \leq 4l$ and $\lvert J \rvert \leq (l- \lvert E \rvert) $. It follows from Lemma \ref{lem1} that $l$ is at most $O(n^2)$. Clearly, the graph defined by the intersection of unit disks centered at points in $V'$ is a unit disk graph. Since both the sets $\lvert V' \rvert $ and $\lvert E' \rvert $ are bounded by $O(n^2)$, we can construct $G'$ from $G$ in polynomial time. 
282:\begin{lemma}\label{lem3}
287:Given a subset $S^{ev}\subseteq E$ and a positive integer $k$, we can verify that $S^{ev}$ is an edge-vertex dominating set of size at most $k$ in polynomial time by checking whether each vertex $v\in V$ is {\it ev}-dominated by an edge $e\in S^{ev}$ all in $O(\lvert V'\rvert \lvert E'\rvert)$ time. 
290:We prove the {\tt NP-hard}ness of the {\it EVDS-UDG} problem by reducing the decision version of the {\it vertex cover} problem on a planar graph with maximum degree 3 to the {\it EVDS-UDG} problem. Let $G=(V,E)$ be a planar graph with a maximum degree of 3. Then from Lemma \ref{lem2}, we can construct an instance $G'=(V',E')$ of {\it EVDS-UDG} in polynomial time.
301:\begin{lemma}\label{lem4}
302:$G$ has a vertex cover of size at most $k$ if and only if $G'$ has an edge-vertex dominating set of size at most $k+l$.
308:{\it Necessary}: Now, for every vertex $v$ in $D'$, choose any one edge of $G'$ that is incident to $v$ where the path does not lead to a pendant vertex in $G$. Represent these chosen edges as the set $N'$. Since $\lvert D'\rvert \leq k$, the cardinality of $N'$ is at most $k$. We can see that for any edge $(p_i, p_j)\in E$ of $G$, we have a simple path in $G'$, consisting of at least one line segment and only added points and joint points between $p_i$ and $p_j$. Let us introduce the notation $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$ to denote this path for any pair of node points $(p_i, p_j)\in E$, where the node point $p_i$ is a vertex in $D'$. Next, traverse every such path exactly once starting at a vertex $v\in D'$, and initially choose the fourth edge from $(v, u)\in N'$ (not counting $(v,u)$) and then on every fourth edge until reaching $p_j$. We repeat this for every vertex $v\in D'$ except for the paths that will lead to pendant vertices of $G$, which will ensure that every path $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$ is traversed exactly once because $D$ is a vertex cover. Similarly, for every path $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$, where $p_i\in D$ and $p_j$ is a pendent vertex, choose the second edge of $G'$ from $p_j$ and continue selecting every fourth edge until reaching $p_i$. Let $N''$ be the set of all these chosen edges. Observe that the edges in $N'\cup N''$ are chosen such that there is at least one edge and at most two edges contained in $N'\cup N''$ for each of $l$ segments (see the darkened edges in Fig. \ref{fig3}(b)). Moreover, the way edges in $N'\cup N''$ are chosen ensures that every two added or joint or node points between any two consecutive of these edges are {\it ev}-dominated by them. The cardinality of $N''$ is $l$ since each line segment consists of at most four added points in the embedding. Therefore, $N'\cup N''$ is an {\it ev}-dominating set for $G'$ and $(\lvert N' \rvert +\lvert N'' \rvert) \leq k+l$.
316:% Now, observe that $S^{ev}=N' \cup N''$ is the set of edges in $G'$ such that every vertex in $G'$ is ev-dominated by at least one edge $e\in S^{ev}$ and $\lvert S^{ev} \rvert \leq 3k+l$. 
318:{\it Sufficiency}: To prove the sufficiency, consider any edge-vertex dominating set $S^{ev}\subseteq E'$ for $G'$, of size at most $k+l$. We know that for any node point $p_i\in V'$, the degree of $p_i$ is at most 3 in both $G$ and $G'$. Let $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_{j_t}$ ($t=1,2,3$) be the three paths in $G'$ as defined above, i.e., all the other vertices through which the path $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_{j_t}$ traverses are only the joint points and added points.
328:% Consider a simple path $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$ in $G'$ between a pair of node points $p_i$ and $p_j$ (say, $p_3\rightsquigarrow p_5$ in Fig. \ref{fig41}) such that the other vertices through which the path $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$ traverses are only the joint points and added points. Now we want to ensure that for any such path either all the incident edges at at least one of $p_i$ and $p_j$ must be part of $S^{ev}$ or none part of $S^{ev}$. Let $\zeta(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j)$ be the subset of edges of $E'$ that appear in the path $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$. For any node point $p_i$ in $G'$, let $\ell_t= \lvert \zeta(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_{j_t}) \rvert \slash 4$ be the number of segments in that path from the embedding for any other node point $p_{j_t}$, where $t\in\{1,2,3\}$. Then, due to the construction of $G'$, we have the following claim (otherwise the point $p_i$ would not be ev-dominated by $S^{ev}$).
334:% Suppose for the node $p_5$ in Figure \ref{fig41}, consider the simple paths $p_5\rightsquigarrow p_3$, $p_5\rightsquigarrow p_6$ and $p_5\rightsquigarrow p_4$. Let the the darken edges be in $S^{ev}$. Then we need to add one more edge to $S^{ev}$ to ev-dominate $p_5$, i.e., either the first or the second edge from $p_5$ (e.g., along $p_5\rightsquigarrow p_3$) so that $p_5$ will be ev-dominated. Thus the Claim \ref{claim1} follows.
338:% \item Now if $\xi=\emptyset$, then pick any node point $p_i$ and the corresponding path $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_{j_t*}$ with $\lvert\zeta(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_{j_t*})\cap S^{ev} \rvert\geq \ell_{t*}+1$. We can introduce the edge from $\zeta(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_{j_t*})$ incident at $p_i$ into $S^{ev}$ and the delete the last edge in the path from $S^{ev}$ and then replace the remaining edges of the path from $S^{ev}$ with their next adjacent ones in the path so that all the vertices which were originally ev-dominated by $\zeta(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_{j_t*})\cap S^{ev}$ remain ev-dominated. 
343:% % Observe that if both $p_i$ and $p_j$ are ev-dominated by only edges of $E(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j)\cap S^{ev}$, then $\lvert E(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j) \rvert \slash 4$ edges are not sufficient to ev-dominate all the vertices of $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$. Hence, the number of edges in $E(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j)\cap S^{ev}$ is greater than $\lvert E(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j) \rvert \slash 4$. Then, we can reselect the same number of edges in $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$ as in  $E(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j)\cap S^{ev}$ and update $S^{ev}$ such that at least one edge of them is now  incident at $p_i$ or $p_j$ (consider the simple path $p_3\rightsquigarrow p_5$ in Figure \ref{fig41}, the darken edges are in $S^{ev}$, then we need to add one more edge to $S^{ev}$ to ev-dominate $p_5$, i.e., either first or second edge from $p_5$ so that $p_5$ will be ev-dominated).
352:% % We can repeat the above process for other such paths in $G'$. However, if both or at least one of $p_i$ and $p_j$ is ev-dominated by edges outside $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$, then we can simply select all the incident edges at one of them and replace the edges in $E(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j)\cap S^{ev}$ with their adjacent ones so that the other endpoint of $p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j$ is ev-dominated. Note that the cardinality of the updated $S^{ev}$ is still at most $3k'+l$, because $\sum_{i,j}(\lvert E(p_i\rightsquigarrow p_j)\cap S^{ev} \rvert -1)\leq l$, where $k'$ is a positive integer.
354:% The updation of $S^{ev}$ takes $O(k\lvert E\rvert)$ time. Now, given such a $S^{ev}$, we can select the vertices in $G$ corresponding to those node points in $G'$, whose all the incident edges are in $S^{ev}$. We are left to argue that this set $D$ ($= \xi)$ of the selected vertices forms a vertex cover of size at most $k$ in $G$. For the sake of contradiction assume that there is an edge $(p_i, p_j)\in E$ which is not covered by $D$. Then it must be the case that neither $p_i\in \xi$ nor $p_j\in \xi$. This will result in either $p_i$ or $p_j$ not being ev-dominated by the updated $S^{ev}$, a contradiction. 
355:The construction of $S^{ev}$ from $D$ and vice versa both take polynomial time. Thus the lemma follows.
358:\begin{theorem}
359:The EVDS-UDG problem is {\tt NP-complete}.
365:\section{Polynomial Time Approximation Scheme}\label{poly}
366:In this section, we propose a PTAS for the {\it EVDS} set problem in a UDG. It is based on the concept of $m$-separated collection of subsets, which was introduced by Nieberg and Hurink \cite{nieb2006}. This concept was used by many other authors to develop PTAS (for e.g., the Roman dominating set \cite{SHAN2007}, minimum Liar's dominating set \cite{JALL2020}, vertex-edge dominating set \cite{jena2020}). However, we adopted that concept here quite differently from these as we have to select edges to dominate vertices in the {\it EVDS} problem. The {\it EVDS} is a domination model with some relevant applications (as we have discussed in Section \ref{intro}) different from other related models such as {\it VEDS}, {\it TDS}, {\it PDS}. Let $G=(V,E)$ be a {\it UDG}. Let $h(e_1,e_2)$ denote the minimum number of edges in a simple path between the endpoints of the edges $e_1$ and $e_2$. Consider any two subsets $E_1\subseteq E$ and $E_2\subseteq E$, $h(E_1,E_2)$ is defined as the minimum number of edges between any two edges $e_1\in E_1$ and $e_2\in E_2$.
367:We use $EVD(A)$ to denote an ev-dominating set and $EVD_{opt}(A)$ to denote the optimal ev-dominating set of the edge-induced subgraph corresponding to $A(\subseteq E)$ (i.e., the subgraph induced by the set of edges $A(\subseteq E)$ and the endpoints of edges in $A$).
371:\begin{lemma} \label{lemp1}
376:Let $A_i$ be the set of edges that are adjacent to edges of $S_i$ for each $i=1,2,\dots,k$ and $R_i$ the set of edges such that $R_i=S_i\cup A_i$. The edges in sets $R_1,R_2,\dots,R_k$ are pairwise disjoint, since the set $S$ is a 4-separated collection of subsets of edges i.e., $(R_i\cap R_j)=\emptyset$, where $i\neq j$. Hence, the edges of $EVD_{opt}(E)\cap R_i$ will ev-dominate every vertex in $S_i$, since $EVD_{opt}(E)$ will ev-dominate every vertex $v\in V$. 
377:On the other hand, also $EVD_{opt}(S_i)\subset R_i$ ev-dominates every vertex of $S_i$, with a minimum number of edges of $G$. This implies that $\vert EVD_{opt}(S_i)\vert \leq \vert EVD_{opt}(E)\cap R_i \vert$. For all $k$ subsets of edges in the 4-separated collection $S$, we get
384:The above Lemma \ref{lemp1} states that a 4-separated collection of subsets of edges $S$ will give a lower bound on the cardinality of an {\it EVDS}. Hence, we can get an approximation for the {\it EVDS} in $G$, if we are able to enlarge $S_i$ to subsets $Q_i\subset E$, in such a way that {\it EVDS} of expansions are bounded locally and dominate every $v\in V$ globally. 
388:\includegraphics[scale=0.65]{approx.eps}
394:\begin{lemma}\label{lemp2}
395:In a graph $G=(V,E)$, let $S=\{S_1,S_2,\dots,S_k \}$ be a 4-separated collection of subsets of edges and $Q=\{Q_1,Q_2,\dots,Q_k \}$ be a collection of subsets of $E$ with $S_i\subseteq Q_i$ for every $i=1,2,\dots,k$. If there is  a $\rho\geq 1$ such that \[ \lvert EVD_{opt}(Q_i) \rvert \leq \rho\lvert EVD_{opt}(S_i) \rvert  \] holds for every $i=1,2,\dots, k$, and if $\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} EVD_{opt}(Q_i)$ is an edge-vertex dominating set of $G$, then $\sum_{i=1}^{k}\lvert EVD_{opt}(Q_i) \rvert $ is a $\rho$-approximation of minimum {\it EVDS} set of $G$.
404:In the following section, we discuss a procedure to construct the subsets $Q_i\subset E$, that contains a 4-separated collection of edges $S_i\subset Q_i$, in such a way that a local $(1+\epsilon)$-approximation can be guaranteed. The union of the respective local {\it EVDS} will ev-dominate the entire vertex set of $G$, which results in a global $(1+\epsilon)$-approximation for the {\it EVDS} problem.
